lessons.concept:
`break` exits the innermost loop; `continue` skips to the next iteration.

Java adds something C lacks: **labelled** break and continue. Placing a label before a loop lets you break out of several levels at once:

```
outer:
for (...) {
    for (...) {
        if (found) break outer;
    }
}
```

That is far clearer than the flag variable the same job requires in C. Use it sparingly — often extracting the loops into a method and returning is cleaner still.

lessons.example_code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int n = 1; n <= 8; n++) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) continue;
            System.out.print(n + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        outer:
        for (int r = 1; r <= 3; r++) {
            for (int c = 1; c <= 3; c++) {
                if (r * c > 4) break outer;
                System.out.print(r * c + " ");
            }
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

